<h2>The case and the illness</h2>
<p>The petitioner is an accused in FIR No. 11/2023, PS Mangol Puri, registered under Sections 365, 302, 394, 397, 201, 120B and 34 of the IPC — kidnapping, murder and robbery with related offences. He has been in judicial custody since 11 January 2023. The medical history is what brought the matter back to the High Court: the petitioner suffers from pulmonary tuberculosis, and doctors have advised surgery.</p>
<p>A six-week interim bail granted on 29 May 2026 lapsed without the operation taking place. When the fresh application was heard, the State handed over an updated report from the thoracic surgery department of the LRS Institute — a report that confirmed the seriousness of the condition and the continuing advice of surgery. The order records no substantive opposition from the prosecution once that report was on record.</p>

<h2>What the court held</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The medical record spoke for itself.</strong> &ldquo;It is evident that the petitioner&rsquo;s medical condition is serious, as is borne out from the aforesaid report,&rdquo; the court noted — the decisive finding, resting on the government hospital&rsquo;s own assessment rather than private medical opinion.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The power is inherent and constitutional.</strong> Relying on the line of authority in <em>Mukesh Kishanpuria v. State of West Bengal</em> and the more recent exposition in <em>Arvind Kejriwal v. Directorate of Enforcement</em>, the court reiterated that the power to grant interim bail &ldquo;is inherent in the power to grant bail, particularly in view of Article 21 of the Constitution of India.&rdquo;</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Twelve weeks, strictly conditioned.</strong> The petitioner was &ldquo;released on interim bail for a period of 12 weeks from the date of his release&rdquo; on a personal bond of Rs 25,000 with one surety, subject to conditions: he must not leave the NCT of Delhi, must not contact prosecution witnesses, must report to the treating institute within one week, must undergo the advised surgery and furnish his hospital records to the investigating officer, and must surrender on expiry.</div>
</div>

<h2>The anatomy of a medical bail order</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Purpose-built duration.</strong> The period is calibrated to the treatment — long enough for surgery and recovery, not an open-ended release. Where an earlier spell proved too short for the procedure, that history itself supports a longer, more realistic window.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Treatment-enforcing conditions.</strong> Directions to report to the hospital, undergo the procedure and file records with the IO ensure the liberty granted is spent on its stated purpose. A release used for anything else invites cancellation and colours every future application.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The State&rsquo;s report matters.</strong> Where the jail&rsquo;s own referral hospital confirms the illness, the usual prosecution objection — that ailments are exaggerated to engineer release — falls away. Candour from the State, as here, shortens the hearing considerably.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Custody since 2023 was not the ground.</strong> The order rests on health, not delay. Long custody and trial delay are separate grounds with their own jurisprudence; conflating them weakens both.</div>
</div>

<h2>The practice points</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Build the application on government-hospital records: referral notes, treatment charts and the specific advice of surgery or specialised care that custody cannot deliver.</p>
  <p>Ask for a period matched to the treatment plan, and explain what happened during any earlier interim release — unexplained lapses are the commonest reason fresh applications fail.</p>
  <p>Offer treatment-verification conditions proactively: reporting to the hospital, filing records with the IO, and a clear surrender date.</p>
  <p>For the prosecution, seek verification from the jail&rsquo;s referral hospital rather than opposing on assertion; the medical record usually decides the matter either way.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">Interim bail on medical grounds is neither an acquittal nor a comment on the merits. It is the system&rsquo;s acknowledgment that imprisonment before conviction cannot lawfully extend to the denial of serious medical care. The twelve-week clock in this case began on release — and ends, as every such order does, at the jail gate.</div>
